# 3. ADVANCED AI CAMERA STOCK COUNTING

This is one of the most important features of the application.

Develop an intelligent camera-based stock-counting system using computer vision and AI object recognition.

The camera must recognize products based on their exact identity, brand, packaging, and configured recognition profile.

## 3.1 Product-specific scanning

The user must first select the product they want to count.

For example:

Selected product: BONITE SODA.

The camera opens and starts recognizing products.

Expected behavior:

- If the camera detects Bonite soda, count it.
- If the camera detects Pepsi, ignore it.
- If the camera detects Coca-Cola, ignore it.
- If the camera detects another beverage, ignore it.
- Only the selected Bonite product can increase the count.

The application must never count every bottle in the camera frame indiscriminately.

### Example: Wine counting

Selected product: BRAND A RED WINE.

The camera must:

- Recognize Brand A Red Wine.
- Count only Brand A Red Wine.
- Ignore Brand B wine.
- Ignore whiskey bottles.
- Ignore beer bottles.
- Ignore soda bottles.
- Ignore other products even when they appear in the same frame.

The user must be able to repeat this process for every product in the inventory.

## 3.2 Product recognition technology

Implement a combination of:

- AI object detection.
- Image classification.
- Optical character recognition (OCR).
- Barcode recognition.
- QR code recognition.
- Product packaging recognition.
- Brand and label recognition.
- Custom-trained product recognition models.

Recommended technologies:
- Google ML Kit.
- TensorFlow Lite or LiteRT.
- CameraX.
- OpenCV.
- Custom machine learning models.

Use appropriate technologies together rather than relying on one recognition method.

## 3.3 Custom product training

Allow administrators to train the application to recognize products that are not already in its database.

For example:

A user adds a new product called:

PRODUCT: KILIMANJARO WATER 1.5L.

The user can:
1. Open product registration.
2. Take several pictures of the product.
3. Capture the front, back, sides, and packaging.
4. Enter the product name.
5. Enter the brand.
6. Enter the size.
7. Save the product.
8. Use it for AI recognition.

The system should build a recognition profile for that product.

Allow administrators to improve recognition using additional photographs when necessary.

Do not claim that a newly registered product can be recognized perfectly without training or validation.

## 3.4 Bottle cap detection

Implement a bottle-condition recognition feature.

For businesses such as bars and lounges, bottles without caps must be excluded from the stock count when the administrator enables the capped-bottle counting rule.

Examples:

- Bonite bottle with cap: COUNT.
- Bonite bottle without cap: IGNORE.
- Pepsi bottle with cap: IGNORE when Bonite is selected.
- Pepsi bottle without cap: IGNORE.
- Other brands: IGNORE.

Important:

The application must distinguish between a bottle being present and a bottle being eligible for counting.

Provide separate configuration options for:
- Count only sealed bottles.
- Count capped bottles.
- Count all physically present bottles.
- Count open bottles separately.

Do not automatically treat every uncapped bottle as empty or damaged.

If the camera cannot clearly determine whether a cap exists, request manual verification instead of making an unreliable assumption.

## 3.5 Prevent duplicate counting

The camera must avoid counting the same physical object repeatedly.

Implement:
- Object tracking.
- Detection confidence thresholds.
- Frame-to-frame tracking.
- Duplicate detection prevention.
- Temporary object tracking IDs.
- Manual correction.

When an object remains in front of the camera, it must not continuously increase the count.

A previously counted object should only become countable again after it leaves the detection area or the user explicitly resets the counting session.

## 3.6 Counting interface

When the user selects a product, display:

- Product photograph.
- Product name.
- Brand.
- Product size.
- Current inventory quantity.
- Live camera preview.
- AI recognition status.
- Detection confidence.
- Automatically detected quantity.
- Manual adjustment buttons.
- Undo button.
- Reset button.
- DONE button.

Example:

Selected product: BONITE SODA 500ML

AI COUNT: 24

MANUAL ADJUSTMENT: + / -

[UNDO] [RESET] [DONE]

When the user presses DONE:

1. Stop the current counting session.
2. Save the verified quantity.
3. Populate the selected product's stock-taking field.
4. Allow manual editing.
5. Save the final confirmed quantity.
6. Return to the product selection screen.
7. Allow the user to select the next product.

Every product must have its own separate counting record.

Never mix quantities from different brands or product categories.

